WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:02.159
It's great to welcome to the program today.

00:00:02.159 --> 00:00:07.420
Daniel Markovits is a professor of law at
the Yale Law School and also author of the

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:14.429
book The Meritocracy Trap How America's Foundational
Myth Feeds Inequality, Dismantles the Middle

00:00:14.429 --> 00:00:16.940
Class and Devours the Elite.

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:19.160
Daniel, so great to have you on today.

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:20.400
It's a real pleasure to be here.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:21.400
Thank you for doing this.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:26.780
So maybe maybe just to start with let's start
with what what maybe is the top line story

00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:32.870
that's often told, which is the great thing
about America is that it's a meritocracy.

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:39.089
Everybody has a chance at making it if they
are whatever enough committed enough if they

00:00:39.089 --> 00:00:43.539
want it enough, regardless of background or
economic status, et cetera.

00:00:43.539 --> 00:00:48.149
How can we evaluate the truth or falsity of
that sort of top line story?

00:00:48.149 --> 00:00:49.149
Yeah.

00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:50.149
Great.

00:00:50.149 --> 00:00:53.020
So what you've done is you've identified what
makes meritocracy so attractive.

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:57.559
It seems like it gives everybody a fair shot
at success if they just have the hard work

00:00:57.559 --> 00:00:58.780
and talent.

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:02.809
And it seems that it produces an elite that's
really capable because it's proved itself

00:01:02.809 --> 00:01:04.870
in competition.

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:08.080
And for a while, meritocracy worked that way
in its early years.

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:11.790
It broke up an old hereditary aristocracy.

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:17.530
But then what happened is that people who
got to the top through meritocracy themselves

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:23.590
turned out to have an almost unbounded taste
and talent for training their children.

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:29.490
And so the elite today invests so much more
in educating its children than anybody else

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:31.630
can possibly afford to do.

00:01:31.630 --> 00:01:36.720
And education works, which means that when
you measure young people based on their academic

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:39.990
accomplishments, it's the kids of rich parents
who do best.

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:45.310
And meritocracy then perpetuates itself and
creates a new kind of aristocracy just based

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:48.440
on degrees rather than breeding.

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:54.010
And that sort of assumes that there is at
least some Zero-Sum nature to elements of

00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:55.340
our society and the economy.

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:56.340
Right.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:59.780
I mean, if one wanted to play devil's advocate,
they could say great.

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:05.510
Once someone through their work achieves,
they become good at training their kids and

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:07.890
their kids are there, then that's fine.

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:12.730
As long as everybody else is not disadvantaged
as a result of that, then it would still be

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:13.730
OK.

00:02:13.730 --> 00:02:14.730
Right?

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:16.500
So I think there are two there are two responses
to that.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:18.040
That's I think that's a powerful point.

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:24.560
The first is there is inevitably some zero
sum competition so that education is a rank

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:25.560
good.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:28.730
And the value of my education depends on how
much education the person behind me in the

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:30.630
job line has.

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:37.360
But the other deeper point is that this new
elite has fundamentally reconstructed the

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:43.680
way our economy works, the way we deliver
services, the way we make goods, so that the

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:49.630
new economy privileges specifically the narrow
set of elite skills that fancy educations

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:56.730
give and devalues all other skills so that
the rich today, when they succeed more, nearly

00:02:56.730 --> 00:03:00.570
push everybody else down than drag draw everybody
else up.

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:09.870
So if we concede that certainly we don't have
a full meritocracy, I think before we explore

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:15.030
some of the specifics in more detail, it might
be interesting to consider would have would

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:18.380
a true meritocracy really be the ideal?

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:21.069
And we can approach that from a few different
perspectives.

00:03:21.069 --> 00:03:22.830
But maybe just I'll start with that question.

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:26.730
What what would be good about an actually
meritocratic society and what might be not

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:27.730
so good?

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:28.730
Yeah.

00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:30.450
So from my perspective, I want to be clear.

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:32.480
A full meritocracy would not be the ideal.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:35.130
It would be more nearly a nightmare.

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:36.890
What would be good about it, though?

00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:43.540
And it has some good things, is that it would
undermine all sorts of hierarchies and inequalities

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:50.940
that are morally awful hierarchies based on
race or caste or gender or sexual orientation

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:52.090
or ethnicity.

00:03:52.090 --> 00:03:54.690
Those are all despised by meritocracy.

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:58.060
And one of the reasons why meritocracy is
so attractive is that it works to undermine

00:03:58.060 --> 00:03:59.060
those.

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:05.620
On the other hand, what it would also do is
it would create a new hierarchy based on training,

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:09.440
based on how much training your parents could
buy for you.

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:12.670
And it would produce incredibly unequal outcomes.

00:04:12.670 --> 00:04:17.100
And if there's one lesson of the book that
I think is really important to drive home,

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:22.960
it's that the idea that equality of opportunity
can make unequal outcomes.

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:23.960
OK.

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:25.330
Is a moral mistake.

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:29.490
And what we need is more equality of outcomes
in our world.

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:31.060
Can you say more about that?

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:35.810
Because it might mean that we have, even though
my audience is mostly on the left.

00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:40.449
I think that there's probably a range of opinions
in terms of the degree to which equality of

00:04:40.449 --> 00:04:43.910
opportunity would be the goal versus equality
of outcome.

00:04:43.910 --> 00:04:48.550
Can you talk a little bit more about because
equality of outcome can mean a lot of different

00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:52.240
things and maybe as to contextualize it.

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:54.520
One of the things that seems immutable in.

00:04:54.520 --> 00:05:06.370
Humanity is that there is rarely exact distribution
in any field or venture that mirrors the makeup

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:11.630
of society, whether it's in terms of gender,
race, age or whatever.

00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:12.630
Right.

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:16.710
So if we start with that reality, equality
of outcome can sound pretty scary in some

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:17.710
ways.

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:18.780
Yeah, sure.

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:25.280
And it's also important to say I'm not and
I don't think anybody's serious is in favor

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:27.710
of absolute equality of outcome.

00:05:27.710 --> 00:05:31.250
You know, the way in which, like pro crustless
used to chop people off if they were too long

00:05:31.250 --> 00:05:33.460
for the bed, he stuck them in that.

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:35.990
That's a deeply dangerous notion.

00:05:35.990 --> 00:05:42.260
But right now, we have so much inequality
around that it's hard really to recognize

00:05:42.260 --> 00:05:43.700
how extreme it is.

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:50.620
The richest one percent of Americans today
control a greater share of the nation's income

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:56.400
and wealth than, for example, a comparable
elite did in ancient Rome during the era of

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:58.639
imperial decadence.

00:05:58.639 --> 00:06:04.430
So we have inequality of outcome that is just
extravagantly skewed.

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:10.530
And when that happens, elites use whatever
selection mechanism there is to privilege

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:11.930
their own kids.

00:06:11.930 --> 00:06:17.360
And so when you get sufficiently big inequality
of outcomes, you can't have equality of opportunity.

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:21.880
Just to give you a way of thinking about that
very quickly in our country today.

00:06:21.880 --> 00:06:26.120
If you take the difference between what a
typical one percent household invest in educating

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:29.680
its children and what a typical middle class
household invests.

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:32.270
So not a poor household, but a middle class
household.

00:06:32.270 --> 00:06:37.240
And every year you take that difference and
you put it in the S&amp;P 500 to create an inheritance

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:39.990
that will go to the rich kids when their parents
die.

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:43.759
That would be, well, north of 10 million dollars
per child.

00:06:43.759 --> 00:06:48.970
And that produces a massive skew in who gets
ahead.

00:06:48.970 --> 00:06:52.490
And it's driven by the inequality of outcomes
in the prior generation.

00:06:52.490 --> 00:06:54.009
And that's unjust.

00:06:54.009 --> 00:06:55.990
Has this been a change in the last 20 or 30
years?

00:06:55.990 --> 00:07:02.090
And by that I mean a change in the direction
of less conspicuous spending by the very rich.

00:07:02.090 --> 00:07:07.870
Let less about things that immediately show
wealth, like what to watch his or expensive

00:07:07.870 --> 00:07:14.130
cars or whatever the case, but a type of spending
on education and upbringing that maybe has

00:07:14.130 --> 00:07:17.410
a more pervasive effect in perpetuating inequality.

00:07:17.410 --> 00:07:19.620
Well, I think two things have happened.

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:23.820
And the underlying driver of this is the rich
have just gotten so much richer.

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:27.830
So one thing that's happened is the rich are
actually spending more on consumption and

00:07:27.830 --> 00:07:30.110
luxury kinds of goods than they used to.

00:07:30.110 --> 00:07:35.199
But because they're not now so many rich people,
they don't stand out in the same way.

00:07:35.199 --> 00:07:36.199
There are whole enclaves.

00:07:36.199 --> 00:07:41.730
There are a whole superstructure is built
to service rich consumption.

00:07:41.730 --> 00:07:46.630
They're increasingly someplace they have separate
airline terminals for the richest passengers.

00:07:46.630 --> 00:07:48.120
So you don't see them.

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:53.690
But it's also the case, as you say, that no
part of consumption inequality has grown faster

00:07:53.690 --> 00:07:55.680
than inequality and spending on education.

00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:57.190
Mm hmm.

00:07:57.190 --> 00:07:59.169
And it's absolutely enormous.

00:07:59.169 --> 00:08:03.250
When we think about so to go back a little
bit, because I think this might be an interesting

00:08:03.250 --> 00:08:04.250
area.

00:08:04.250 --> 00:08:07.060
And, oh, I should mention, we've been speaking
with Daniel Markovits.

00:08:07.060 --> 00:08:11.620
We're gonna go to a break on the TV, radio
and podcast episode, but the conversation

00:08:11.620 --> 00:18:40.580
will continue in full on our YouTube channel,

